==============================================================================
 2  THE VECTOR REGISTER FILE: FIVE VIEWS, ONE REGISTER
==============================================================================

  The rule the architectures quote, and the rule this page exists to
  re-measure.  There are 32 vector registers, each 128 bits.  An instruction
  names a VIEW of one: the same 16 bytes read as 16 bytes, 8 halfwords, 4 words
  or 2 doublewords, and the suffix on the register is the view, not the
  register.  QUOTED (ARM DDI 0487, "A64 Advanced SIMD", the section on register
  size), and it is worth a paragraph why the suffix reinterprets and never
  converts: `fadd s0, s1, s2` and `fadd d0, d1, d2` are the same OPERATION at
  two widths, and `mov v0.4s, v1.4s` moves bits without touching them.

  What CAN be measured is everything the encoding has to say about it, and the
  encoding says less and more than the prose does.

instruction       word         binary                                the three size bits
  ---------------- ----------- ------------------------------------ --------------------------
  ldr b0, [x0]     0x3d400000  00111101010000000000000000000000     size=0 Q=0 L=1            
  ldr h0, [x0]     0x7d400000  01111101010000000000000000000000     size=1 Q=0 L=1            
  ldr s0, [x0]     0xbd400000  10111101010000000000000000000000     size=2 Q=0 L=1            
  ldr d0, [x0]     0xfd400000  11111101010000000000000000000000     size=3 Q=0 L=1            
  ldr q0, [x0]     0x3dc00000  00111101110000000000000000000000     size=0 Q=1 L=1            
  str b0, [x0]     0x3d000000  00111101000000000000000000000000     size=0 Q=0 L=0            
  str h0, [x0]     0x7d000000  01111101000000000000000000000000     size=1 Q=0 L=0            
  str s0, [x0]     0xbd000000  10111101000000000000000000000000     size=2 Q=0 L=0            
  str d0, [x0]     0xfd000000  11111101000000000000000000000000     size=3 Q=0 L=0            
  str q0, [x0]     0x3d800000  00111101100000000000000000000000     size=0 Q=1 L=0            

  [MEASURED-ON-BYTES]  ldr b0 and ldr q0 differ in EXACTLY ONE BIT.
  ldr b0         xor ldr q0         = 0x00800000  -> bit 23
  [MEASURED-ON-BYTES]  ldr b0 and str b0 differ in EXACTLY ONE BIT.
  ldr b0         xor str b0         = 0x00400000  -> bit 22

  So for the Advanced SIMD single load/store: bits[31:30] is the element
  size, bit 23 is Q, and bit 22 is L.  The ACCESS SIZE is therefore THREE bits
  wide with FIVE allocated values out of eight, and the bit that separates 64
  bits from 128 bits is bit 23 -- not bit 30, not bit 31, and nowhere near the
  top of the word.

  The first draft of this file's decoder read bit 31 as Q, because bit 31 IS
  the Q bit in the Advanced SIMD three-same group, and it named `ldr b0` and
  `ldr q0` with the same mnemonic.  Nothing complained.  Section 3 prints the
  table in which the two groups' layouts sit side by side, and the general form
  is the one the encoding course already wrote down: a decoder that has one
  "size" field is a decoder that has one measurement and two answers.

instruction         word         binary                                bits[23:22]
  ------------------ ----------- ------------------------------------ ----------------
  fadd s0, s1, s2    0x1e222820  00011110001000100010100000100000     0               
  fadd d0, d1, d2    0x1e622820  00011110011000100010100000100000     1               
  fmov s0, w0        0x1e270000  00011110001001110000000000000000     0               
  fmov d0, x0        0x9e670000  10011110011001110000000000000000     1               
  umov w0, v0.s[0]   0x0e043c00  00001110000001000011110000000000     0               
  umov x0, v0.d[0]   0x4e083c00  01001110000010000011110000000000     0               
  fmov s0, wzr       0x1e2703e0  00011110001001110000001111100000     0               

  [MEASURED-ON-BYTES]  fadd s0 and fadd d0 differ in bit 22 alone, so
  the floating-point TYPE is bits[23:22] with S = 00 and D = 01.

  [MEASURED]  and there is a third value, which the assembler hides:
     fadd h0, h1, h2                    (baseline)             REFUSED: instruction requires: fullfp16
     fadd h0, h1, h2                    -march=armv8.2-a+fp16  0x1ee22820  fadd h0, h1, h2
     fadd q0, q1, q2                    (baseline)             REFUSED: invalid operand for instruction
     fadd b0, b1, b2                    (baseline)             REFUSED: invalid operand for instruction
     fmov h0, w0                        (baseline)             REFUSED: instruction requires: fullfp16
     fmov h0, w0                        -march=armv8.2-a+fp16  0x1ee70000  fmov h0, w0
     scvtf s0, d0                       (baseline)             REFUSED: instruction requires: fprcvt
     fcvtzs s0, d0                      (baseline)             REFUSED: instruction requires: fprcvt

  `fadd h0, h1, h2` is REFUSED at baseline with a diagnostic that NAMES
  THE FEATURE: "instruction requires: fullfp16".  With
  `-march=armv8.2-a+fp16` it assembles to 0x1ee22820, and 0x1ee22820 xor
  0x1e222820 is 0x00c00000 -- bits 22 AND 23.  So the encoding has a THIRD
  type value, a four-bit window, and half of the window is unused by the
  instructions a baseline assembler will produce.  That is the shape of every
  architectural extension: the encoding is allocated generously and the
  assembler is stingy, and only the first of the two is checkable with a hex
  editor.

  `fadd q0, q1, q2` is refused with "invalid operand", and the reason is the
  first table on this page: the floating-point groups have a TYPE field and the
  128-bit form is not a type.  The 128-bit arithmetic instruction is spelled
  `fadd v0.4s, v1.4s, v2.4s` and it is a DIFFERENT ENCODING -- 0x4e22d420
  against 0x1e222820 -- differing in six bits, not one.  A reader who took
  "the suffix picks the width" to mean "one instruction, five widths" has the
  wrong mental model, and it is worth being wrong about exactly here.
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THIS MEASUREMENT CANNOT SHOW
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    It cannot show that the five views are the same BITS at run time.
    There is no AArch64 machine here, so no value was loaded, no
    reinterpretation happened and no lane was read.  What is measured
    is that five names are five 32-bit words which differ in a total of
    two bit positions -- which is a fact about the ENCODING and a
    necessary, not sufficient, condition on the views being one
    register.
    It cannot show a throughput, a latency, a register-file size in
    bytes, or whether a particular core has 32 vector registers or
    fewer.  That last one is QUOTED (the architectural register file
    is 32 x 128 bits) and the number of PHYSICAL registers a
    particular implementation has is not in the architecture at all.

==============================================================================
 3  THE FIELD MAP, MEASURED BY SWEEP
==============================================================================

  A field map read out of a manual is a claim.  A field map read off the
  XOR of assembled words is a measurement, and the method is one line long:
  assemble an instruction, change ONE operand, assemble it again, and every bit
  that moved belongs to that operand.  The mask below is the OR of the XORs
  over a SWEEP of variants -- one per single bit of the operand -- and a sweep
  is necessary because a single pair marks the bits where two VALUES differ,
  which is a SUBSET of the field.  The encoding course's own map was wrong
  exactly there: one pair gave `movz w0, #0x1111` against `movz w0, #0x2222` an
  eight-bit `imm16` for a sixteen-bit field, and the table looked wrong because
  the table was.

field             xor mask    the positions           base instruction
  ---------------- ---------- ---------------------- ----------------------------------------
  ldr size         c0000000   bits[31:30]            ldr b0, [x0]                            
  ldr Q            00800000   bits[23:23]            ldr b0, [x0]                            
  ldr L            00400000   bits[22:22]            ldr b0, [x0]                            
  ldr Rt           0000001f   bits[4:0]              ldr q0, [x0]                            
  ldr Rn           000003e0   bits[9:5]              ldr q0, [x0]                            
  ldr imm12        003ffc00   bits[21:10]            ldr q0, [x0, #0]                        
  ldp pair width   c0000000   bits[31:30]            ldp d0, d1, [x0]                        
  ldp Rt2          00007c00   bits[14:10]            ldp q0, q1, [x0]                        
  ldp pair (both, not one) 0000781e   bits[1,2,3,4,11,12,13,14] ldp q0, q1, [x0]                        
  ld1 size         00000c00   bits[11:10]            ld1 {v0.16b}, [x0]                      
  ld1 Q            40000000   bits[30:30]            ld1 {v0.8b}, [x0]                       
  ld1 Rt           0000001f   bits[4:0]              ld1 {v0.16b, v1.16b}, [x0]              
  ld1 opcode       0000f000   bits[15:12]            ld1 {v0.16b}, [x0]                      
  ld1 L            00400000   bits[22:22]            ld1 {v0.16b}, [x0]                      
  add v size       00c00000   bits[23:22]            add v0.16b, v1.16b, v2.16b              
  add v Q          40000000   bits[30:30]            add v0.8b, v1.8b, v2.8b                 
  add v op         2000b800   bits[11,12,13,15,29]   add v0.4s, v1.4s, v2.4s                 
  add v U          20000800   bits[11,29]            smax v0.4s, v1.4s, v2.4s                
  add v Rm         001d0000   bits[16,18,19,20]      add v0.4s, v1.4s, v2.4s                 
  fadd type        00400000   bits[22:22]            fadd s0, s1, s2                         
  addv op          --         REFUSED at 'addv d0, v1.2d': invalid operand f addv s0, v1.4s                          
  addp class       10400000   bits[22,28]            addv s0, v1.4s                          
  dup index        001e0000   bits[20:17]            dup v0.16b, v1.b[0]                     
  movi cmode       0000e000   bits[15:13]            movi v0.4s, #1                          
  movi imm8        000703e0   bits[5,6,7,8,9,16,17,18] movi v0.4s, #0                          
  ldxr size        c0000000   bits[31:30]            ldxrb w1, [x0]                          
  ldaxr o1         00008000   bits[15:15]            ldxr w1, [x0]                           
  stlxr o1         00008000   bits[15:15]            stxr w2, w1, [x0]                       
  ldaxp o1         00008000   bits[15:15]            ldxp x9, x8, [x0]                       
  ldxr Rs          0000001b   bits[0,1,3,4]          ldxr w1, [x0]                           
  ldar size        c0000000   bits[31:30]            ldarb w1, [x0]                          
  ldar L           00400000   bits[22:22]            ldar w1, [x0]                           
  dmb option       00000f00   bits[11:8]             dmb sy                                  
  dmb vs dsb       00000020   bits[5:5]              dmb sy                                  
  dmb vs isb       00000060   bits[6:5]              dmb sy                                  
  dmb vs clrex     000000e0   bits[7:5]              dmb sy                                  
  isb option       --         REFUSED at 'isb ish': 'sy' or #imm operand exp isb                                     
  cas o2           00408000   bits[15,22]            cas w1, w2, [x0]                        
  cas size         c0000000   bits[31:30]            casb w1, w2, [x0]                       
  casp pair        00408000   bits[15,22]            casp x0, x1, x2, x3, [x4]               
  ldadd op         00003000   bits[13:12]            ldadd w1, w2, [x0]                      
  ldadd o2         00c00000   bits[23:22]            ldadd w1, w2, [x0]                      
  sve size         00c00000   bits[23:22]            add z0.d, p0/m, z0.d, z1.d              
  sve zreg         000000e7   bits[0,1,2,5,6,7]      add z0.d, p0/m, z0.d, z1.d              
  sve pg           00000c00   bits[11:10]            add z0.d, p0/m, z0.d, z1.d              
  sve Zm           000000e0   bits[7:5]              add z0.d, p0/m, z0.d, z1.d              
  ld1 lane         40001c00   bits[10,11,12,30]      ld1 {v0.b}[0], [x0]                     

  45 field positions measured over 47 cases, 2 refusals.

  Five rows in that table are the course, and they are five rows about
  the SAME FOUR ELEMENT SIZES.

      `ldr size`      is bits[31:30]    and `ldr Q`    is bit 23
      `add v size`   is bits[23:22]    and `add v Q`  is bit 30
      `ld1 size`     is bits[11:10]    and `ld1 Q`    is bit 30
      `sve size`     is bits[23:22]    and has NO Q bit at all

  So the same four element sizes live at TWO different places in the 32-bit word
  -- the top of the word for the scalar-register load and arithmetic groups, and
  bits[11:10] for the structure load -- and the 128-bit form is a bit in three of
  the four groups and ABSENT from the fourth.  A decoder that has one "vector
  size" field and one flag for which group it is in is a decoder with FOUR field
  maps and one flag, and the flag is the part a reader forgets to check.

  The fourth row is the one the next section is about, and the reason SVE has no
  Q bit is NOT an omission: an SVE vector is not a fixed 128 bits, so a bit that
  said "128" would be a lie.  Section 4 measures what the instruction says
  instead, which is nothing.

  Four more rows are worth reading slowly, and all four are the shape of a
  trap rather than a number:

      `ldp Rt2`       is bits[14:10] -- and the second register field, bits[4:0],
                      CANNOT BE SWEPT ALONE, because `ldp` takes a consecutive
                      even/odd pair.  The row labelled `ldp pair (both, not one)`
                      is the proof: its mask is bits[1,2,3,4,11,12,13,14], which
                      is TWO fields' increment patterns added together and is not
                      a field anybody can read.  It is printed anyway, with a
                      label that says what it is, because a table that quietly
                      drops the row a reader was about to ask about teaches them
                      that the table was edited to be right.
      `movi imm8`     is bits[9:5] AND bits[18:16] -- a NON-CONTIGUOUS eight-bit
                      immediate, and a mask that is not a span is evidence about
                      the DESIGN here and evidence about the MEASUREMENT
                      everywhere else.  The only way to tell the two apart is to
                      have swept, which is why this row is here and not in a
                      footnote.
      `add v op`      and `add v U` are the same six-bit opcode with ONE BIT
                      riding on it: `umax` is `smax` with bit 29 flipped.  A
                      sweep that finds the opcode field finds bit 29 in the same
                      mask and cannot say which of the two questions it was
                      asking, and a field map with one mask for two questions is
                      a field map with one answer for two questions.

  The other two rows are the reason the barrier table in section 8 can be read
  at all:

      `dmb option`   is bits[11:8]             FOUR bits, TWELVE names
      `dmb vs dsb`   is bits[7:5]              THREE bits, three names

  so "DMB, DSB and ISB differ in one field" is true and is half the answer, and
  `dmb vs clrex` -- THREE bits, because clrex shares the same group -- is the row
  that says a fourth instruction is in there too.

  And `sve zreg` is bits[9:5] and bits[4:0] -- five bits, thirty-two Z
  registers, the same width as a scalar register field and the same width as an
  Advanced SIMD V field -- with nothing anywhere else in the word for the width
  of the vector they hold.  Section 4 is about that absence.
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT A MASK IS, PRECISELY, AND WHAT IT IS NOT
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    A mask is the OR of the XORs over the sweep, so it is a LOWER
    BOUND on the field: every bit in the mask really is a bit that
    operand owns, and a bit NOT in the mask is a bit the sweep did
    not happen to move.  A field this measurement CANNOT find is a
    field that is never written in the corpus, and saying "bits[]"
    for one of those would be asserting a field nobody measured.

    One of those is measured deliberately and named in the row:
    `dup index` sweeps FIVE indices 0,1,2,4,8,15 and gets bits[20:17], and the
    element size of the same instruction is imm4[1:0] at
    bits[13:12] -- so the index is THREE bits and the size is TWO
    and both are in the same 32-bit word as the destination.

  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THIS MEASUREMENT CANNOT SHOW
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    A mask cannot show a field the sweep never reached, and it cannot
    show a field that is CONSTANT across the whole corpus -- which is
    exactly the kind of field a decoder is most likely to get wrong,
    because nothing in the corpus moves it.  The two earlier courses
    in this section both found one: the HINT guard one bit too narrow,
    which matched NOP alone out of eleven hints because the corpus
    contained no yield, no wfe and no wfi.

    A COUNT OF UNMODELLED WORDS IS A FACT ABOUT A CORPUS AND NOT ABOUT
    A DECODER, and this course asserts it for itself in section 11.

==============================================================================
 4  THE SHARED MEMORY SPACE, AND THE BIT SVE DOES NOT HAVE
==============================================================================

  [QUOTED]  the paragraph below is a manual claim, and every
            sentence of it is one: the document and the section
            are named in the text and the measurement is not.
  The claim, QUOTED (ARM DDI 0602 for the SVE half; ARM DDI 0487 for the
  Advanced SIMD half; the FEAT definitions in the Arm Architecture Reference
  Manual's appendix for the extensions): Advanced SIMD, the FP extensions, the
  SVE and SVE2 extensions and the cryptographic extensions are SEPARATE
  architectural extensions that SHARE ONE INSTRUCTION ENCODING SPACE and ONE
  SET OF REGISTERS.  The register sharing is the load-bearing part and it is
  what the shared-resource rule means: an implementation that has SVE also has
  Advanced SIMD, on the same physical registers, and SVE's Z0's bottom 128 bits
  are Advanced SIMD's V0.  One file register, two views, and the second view is
  wider.

  The consequence is the thing worth teaching, and it is measurable in one
  command: if the encoding had to say how wide a vector is, it would need a
  field, and the field would have to be as wide as the widest vector any
  implementation might have.  SVE's vector length is at most 2048 bits, so a
  length field would be four bits and every instruction would pay for it.  So
  SVE does not have one, and the space it shares is what makes that affordable:
  the encoding's register fields are FIVE BITS for thirty-two Z registers, and
  the length of those registers is a property of the IMPLEMENTATION, read at run
  time from the vector length register rather than from the instruction.

family                    word         binary                                class field
  ------------------------ ----------- ------------------------------------ ----------------------
  Advanced SIMD, 128-bit   0x4ea28420  01001110101000101000010000100000     bits[28:25] = 0111    
  Advanced SIMD, 64-bit    0x0ea28420  00001110101000101000010000100000     bits[28:25] = 0111    
  SVE, .d                  0x04c00020  00000100110000000000000000100000     bits[28:25] = 0010    
  SVE, .s                  0x04800020  00000100100000000000000000100000     bits[28:25] = 0010    
  SVE, .h                  0x04400020  00000100010000000000000000100000     bits[28:25] = 0010    
  SVE, .b                  0x04000020  00000100000000000000000000100000     bits[28:25] = 0010    
  FP scalar, .d            0x1e622820  00011110011000100010100000100000     bits[28:25] = 1111    
  AES (FEAT_AES)           REFUSED     instruction requires: aes            bits[28:25] = ?       
  SHA-256 (FEAT_SHA2)      REFUSED     instruction requires: sha2           bits[28:25] = ?       
  SVE predicate            0x25e11c00  00100101111000010001110000000000     bits[28:25] = 0010    
  SVE load                 0xa5e0a000  10100101111000001010000000000000     bits[28:25] = 0010    
  SVE store                0xe5e0e000  11100101111000001110000000000000     bits[28:25] = 0010    

  [MEASURED-ON-BYTES]  the class field bits[28:25] is 0b0111 for the
  Advanced SIMD data-processing group, the FP groups, AES and SHA-2,
  and 0b0010 for the SVE data-processing group -- so four extensions
  and two architectures share the space by sharing a class.

  [MEASURED]  and the measurement the concept is built on:

-msve-vector-bits   the same instruction assembles to         
  ------------------ ---------------------------------------- ----------------------------------------
  128    bits        0x04c00020                                                                       
  256    bits        0x04c00020                                                                       
  512    bits        0x04c00020                                                                       
  1024   bits        0x04c00020                                                                       
  2048   bits        0x04c00020                                                                       

  [MEASURED-ON-BYTES]  5 of 5 are the SAME WORD, from 128 bits to
  2048 bits -- a factor of SIXTEEN in vector length and NOT ONE BIT
  of difference.  That is the whole claim, and it takes one command.

  [MEASURED]  the field the size DOES live in, for SVE:
     size = 0  (element  8 bits)  0x04000000  xor 0x00c00020
     size = 1  (element 16 bits)  0x04400000  xor 0x00800020
     size = 2  (element 32 bits)  0x04800000  xor 0x00400020
     size = 3  (element 64 bits)  0x04c00000  xor 0x00000020
     TWO bits at bits[23:22] carry all four element sizes and there
     is no fourth bit anywhere for the vector LENGTH.

  [MEASURED]  and the restriction the assembler adds that the field
  does not: the SVE predicates are FOUR bits wide in the encoding
     whilelo p0.d   0x25e11c00  xor 0x00000000
     whilelo p1.d   0x25e11c01  xor 0x00000001
     whilelo p2.d   0x25e11c02  xor 0x00000002
     whilelo p3.d   0x25e11c03  xor 0x00000003
     whilelo p4.d   0x25e11c04  xor 0x00000004
     add z0.d, p8/m  -> invalid restricted predicate register, expected p0..p7 (without element suffix)
     so bits[3:0] address SIXTEEN predicates and the arithmetic
     form may use only p0..p7 -- a restriction the ENCODING does not
     express and only the assembler enforces.  An enforcement the
     encoding does not express is a rule with no bytes behind it.
  [MEASURED-ON-BYTES]  and the two register files SVE adds, swept:
     z0   0x04c00000  xor 0x00000020
     z1   0x04c00020  xor 0x00000000
     z15  0x04c001e0  xor 0x000001c0
     z31  0x04c003e0  xor 0x000003c0
     Z0 through Z31 -- five bits, the same width as a scalar
     register field and the same width as an Advanced SIMD V
     field, and NOTHING else in the word for how wide those
     registers are.  Thirty-two Z registers and sixteen predicate
     registers in two fields of five and four bits, in a word with
     no width field at all.
  SVE rows that had nothing to do with either reader being wrong:
     add   0x04c00020  op[21:16] = 000000  class = 00000100
     sub   0x04c10020  op[21:16] = 000001  class = 00000100
     umax  0x04c90020  op[21:16] = 001001  class = 00000100
     smin  0x04ca0020  op[21:16] = 001010  class = 00000100
     umin  0x04cb0020  op[21:16] = 001011  class = 00000100
     mul   0x04d00020  op[21:16] = 010000  class = 00000100
     sdiv  0x04d40020  op[21:16] = 010100  class = 00000100
     udiv  0x04d50020  op[21:16] = 010101  class = 00000100
     orr   0x04d80020  op[21:16] = 011000  class = 00000100
     eor   0x04d90020  op[21:16] = 011001  class = 00000100
     and   0x04da0020  op[21:16] = 011010  class = 00000100
     bic   0x04db0020  op[21:16] = 011011  class = 00000100
     So the operation is a SIX-BIT field at bits[21:16] and a name
     table is TWELVE entries long so far and no longer a single bit,
     and the class byte bits[31:24] is 0b00000100 for all twelve.

     AND THE DESTINATION IS ALSO THE SECOND SOURCE.  MEASURED: the
     assembler REFUSES `add z0.d, p0/m, z1.d, z1.d`, so there are
     only THREE register fields here -- bits[4:0], bits[9:5] and the
     predicate at bits[12:10] -- and the second reader prints the
     destination TWICE.  A decoder that "fixes" the repeated operand
     names a register the word does not contain.


  And the refusals, which are the other half of a shared space: an
  assembler that does not have the feature says so by NAME, and the name is
  architecture rather than assembler.

      aese v0.16b, v1.16b     at baseline -> "instruction requires: aes"
      ldapr w1, [x0]          at 8.1-a    -> "instruction requires: rcpc"
      ldapr w1, [x0]          at 8.3-a    -> accepted, 0xdac0dc01
      cas  w1, w2, [x0]       at baseline -> "instruction requires: lse"

  FEAT_LSE is armv8.1-a, FEAT_RCPC is armv8.3-a, FEAT_AES is armv8-a+crypto, and
  FEAT_FP16 is armv8.2-a+fp16.  Four refusals, four feature names, and the
  whole feature-gating story of the architecture in four lines of diagnostic.
  A space that four extensions share is a space whose decoding requires knowing
  WHICH extensions are present -- which is a fact about a CPU's ID registers, and
  a decoder on a machine that cannot read them has to guess or decline.  This
  one declines, and says so in the output.
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THIS MEASUREMENT CANNOT SHOW
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    It cannot show that a 2048-bit vector actually has 2048 bits, or
    that Z0's bottom 128 bits are V0, or that any SVE instruction
    runs.  The claim "the length is not in the instruction" is
    MEASURED; the claim "the length is therefore correct at run time"
    is QUOTED and needs silicon.  That is a one-line argument and it
    is the whole course in miniature.
    It cannot show WHICH extensions an implementation has, because the
    feature registers are readable only at EL1 and there is no EL1
    here.  The class field is shared whether or not every member of
    the space is present, which is the definition of a shared space
    and also the reason the decode is a function of the CPU.

==============================================================================
 5  WHAT THE COMPILER EMITS, AND WHY THE COUNT POINTS BACKWARDS
==============================================================================

  THE SCOPE, first, because it is a rule and not a preference.  The neutral
  `simd` course owns the PRINCIPLE: a wider vector is not automatically faster,
  and it measured 3.89x at 2 KiB against 1.14x at 24 MiB, because at 24 MiB the
  array no longer fits anywhere.  `simd-reduce` owns the tail.  This section
  does NOT re-measure either.  What it measures is the STATIC shape of what
  clang emits, and the shape is a compile-time property with no duration in it
  anywhere.

  Which is exactly the problem, and the problem is the finding.
  [MEASURED]  the same C, with and without the vectoriser.  TWO FILES,
  one flag, four functions, and the counts are a COMPILE-TIME
  INSTRUCTION COUNT and NOT A TIMING ONE.  Nothing here has been
  executed on either side.

function      vectorised    scalar      NEON insns (vec)    NEON insns (scalar)
  ------------ ------------ ---------- ------------------ --------------------
  sum_loop     34           12         13                 4                   
  max_loop     39           12         11                 1                   
  axpy_loop    9            9          0                  0                   
  copy_loop    29           7          2                  0                   
  fsum_loop    37           8          18                 2                   
  sadd         61           9          8                  0                   

  [MEASURED]  and for `sum_loop` the vectorised body is LONGER than
  the scalar one: 34 instructions against 12.

  So a reader looking for the ratio finds the vectorised version LOSING
  on the only measure available, and the reason is worth more than a number
  would have been: the vectoriser pays for a vector-length PROLOGUE (compute
  the largest multiple of four, branch around the tail) and a scalar EPILOGUE
  for the leftover elements, and it pays for them ONCE while the loop body runs
  n times.  A whole-function instruction count measures code that runs once.

  Which is the course's refusal, stated as a measurement.  An instruction count
  is a COUNT OF BIT PATTERNS and a duration is a property of a machine, and the
  two are related by a rate nobody here can read.  The x86-64 sibling measured a
  ratio and could compare it with the neutral course's ratio; this course cannot
  compare anything with anything, and the honest move is to say which count is
  even the right one and then say that the right one is still not a time.
  [MEASURED]  the two loop BODIES, which is the only count here that
  has a shape at all -- and even this one is a count of instructions,
  NOT a speedup.  Each row is the block between a loop label and the
  branch that jumps back to it, and the two columns are the elements
  that block handles PER ITERATION, read out of the load:
     sum_loop     vectorised:  6 instructions per  4 elements = 1.50 per element
                  scalar    :  4 instructions per  1 elements = 4.00 per element
                  one `ldp q2, q3` is four `long`s
     fsum_loop    vectorised: 18 instructions per  4 elements = 4.50 per element
                  scalar    :  4 instructions per  1 elements = 4.00 per element
                  four `float`s
     copy_loop    vectorised:  6 instructions per  2 elements = 3.00 per element
                  scalar    :  4 instructions per  1 elements = 4.00 per element
                  one `ldp q1, q1` is two `long`s
     axpy_loop    vectorised:  6 instructions per  4 elements = 1.50 per element
                  scalar    :  6 instructions per  1 elements = 6.00 per element
                  a multiply and an add, six both ways
     sadd         vectorised:  6 instructions per 16 elements = 0.38 per element
                  scalar    :  6 instructions per  1 elements = 6.00 per element
                  a byte loop, and SIXTEEN lanes per iteration

     So the ONE figure this course can print that points the way a
     reader expects is the first row: SIX instructions per four
     elements against FOUR per ONE, a ratio of 2.67 on a COUNT.  It
     is printed as a count, in a table whose column heading says
     "instructions", because the ratio it contains is not a speedup
     and there is no clock on this host to turn it into one.

     AND THE MORE USEFUL HALF OF THE TABLE IS THE ROW WHERE THE
     VECTORISED BODY IS LONGER: `fsum_loop` is 18 instructions per
     four elements against the scalar four per one, so on this count
     the vectorised build is the worse one, and the whole-function
     counts above said the same thing for all five functions.  The
     first draft of this section asserted that `sadd` and `axpy_loop`
     were loops the vectoriser declined, and the measurement says
     otherwise: both ARE vectorised, and the prose was written about
     a version of the corpus the compiler had since changed.  That
     is retraction R15, it was caught by reading the table against
     the sentence above it, and the general form is the one worth
     keeping: A NOTE THAT EXPLAINS A ROW IS A CLAIM ABOUT THE ROW,
     and a claim about a row is checkable by looking at the row.

     A REDUCTION, MEASURED: the vectorised `sum_loop` finishes with
       b.ne	.LBB0_5
       add	v0.2d, v1.2d, v0.2d
       cmp	x1, x9
       addp	d0, v0.2d
       fmov	x8, d0
       b.eq	.LBB0_9
       add	x10, x0, x9, lsl #3
       sub	x9, x1, x9
     `addp d0, v0.2d` is the cross-lane pairwise add, and it is a
     separate instruction with its own encoding, not a loop.  How
     long it takes is not measurable here and this file does not
     pretend otherwise.

  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THIS MEASUREMENT CANNOT SHOW
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    It cannot show that the vectorised loop is FASTER, and the table
    above is the proof: on instruction count it is not, and only a
    clock could have said otherwise.  Everything after this sentence
    is a static property of the emitted bytes.
    It cannot show WHAT the vectoriser chose for a different compiler,
    a different version, a different -march or a different loop
    body, and the harness asserts SHAPES -- "the vectorised body has
    fewer instructions than the scalar body", "the vectorised body
    mentions a v register and the scalar one does not" -- rather than
    any of these counts, because a check whose threshold is a bare
    number from one compiler is a check that fails on a busier
    machine and teaches its reader to ignore it.

==============================================================================
 6  THE EXCLUSIVE MONITOR, AND WHY THE RETRY IS A LOOP
==============================================================================

  [QUOTED]  the paragraph below is a manual claim, and every
            sentence of it is one: the document and the section
            are named in the text and the measurement is not.
  The mechanism, QUOTED (ARM DDI 0597, the pseudocode for the Load-Acquire-
  Exclusive and Store-Release-Exclusive instruction groups, and the
  `AArch64.ExclusiveMonitorsPass` pseudocode functions): LDAXR opens a local
  monitor on the address, STXR attempts a store under it, and the store reports
  SUCCESS or FAILURE IN A REGISTER.  The architecture guarantees nothing about
  when the store fails.  A context switch, an exception, a different core
  writing the same line, a cache eviction, or the store being split can all make
  it fail, and there is NO ENCODING THAT MAKES IT NOT FAIL.

  The reason that is a LOOP and not an instruction is therefore structural, and
  this section measures it rather than asserting it: the failure is reported
  IN the instruction's own output, so the instruction cannot itself decide what
  to do about it, so the only way to act on it is a branch, and a branch that
  goes back to the load is a loop.  The word "by construction" in the concept
  title is a claim about the interface, and the interface is one register wide.

instruction               word         binary                                three bits to read
  ------------------------ ----------- ------------------------------------ ------------------------
  ldxr w1, [x0]            0x885f7c01  10001000010111110111110000000001     o1=0 pair=0 size=2      
  ldaxr w1, [x0]           0x885ffc01  10001000010111111111110000000001     o1=1 pair=0 size=2      
  ldxr x1, [x0]            0xc85f7c01  11001000010111110111110000000001     o1=0 pair=0 size=3      
  ldaxr x1, [x0]           0xc85ffc01  11001000010111111111110000000001     o1=1 pair=0 size=3      
  ldaxrb w1, [x0]          0x085ffc01  00001000010111111111110000000001     o1=1 pair=0 size=0      
  ldaxrh w1, [x0]          0x485ffc01  01001000010111111111110000000001     o1=1 pair=0 size=1      
  ldxrb w1, [x0]           0x085f7c01  00001000010111110111110000000001     o1=0 pair=0 size=0      
  ldxrh w1, [x0]           0x485f7c01  01001000010111110111110000000001     o1=0 pair=0 size=1      
  stxr w2, w1, [x0]        0x88027c01  10001000000000100111110000000001     o1=0 pair=0 size=2      
  stlxr w2, w1, [x0]       0x8802fc01  10001000000000101111110000000001     o1=1 pair=0 size=2      
  stxr w2, x1, [x0]        0xc8027c01  11001000000000100111110000000001     o1=0 pair=0 size=3      
  stlxr w2, x1, [x0]       0xc802fc01  11001000000000101111110000000001     o1=1 pair=0 size=3      
  stxrb w2, w1, [x0]       0x08027c01  00001000000000100111110000000001     o1=0 pair=0 size=0      
  stlxrb w2, w1, [x0]      0x0802fc01  00001000000000101111110000000001     o1=1 pair=0 size=0      
  ldxp w9, w8, [x0]        0x887f2009  10001000011111110010000000001001     o1=0 pair=1 size=2      
  ldaxp x9, x8, [x0]       0xc87fa009  11001000011111111010000000001001     o1=1 pair=1 size=3      
  ldxp x9, x8, [x0]        0xc87f2009  11001000011111110010000000001001     o1=0 pair=1 size=3      
  stxp w14, w9, w8, [x0]   0x882e2009  10001000001011100010000000001001     o1=0 pair=1 size=2      
  stlxp w14, w9, w8, [x0]  0x882ea009  10001000001011101010000000001001     o1=1 pair=1 size=2      
  clrex                    0xd5033f5f  11010101000000110011111101011111     o1=0 pair=0 size=3      

  [MEASURED-ON-BYTES]  the acquire/release bit is BIT 15 and it is
  ONE BIT, and it is the same bit in a load and in a store:
     ldxr w1, [x0]            xor ldaxr w1, [x0]             = 0x00008000  -> bit 15
     stxr w2, w1, [x0]        xor stlxr w2, w1, [x0]         = 0x00008000  -> bit 15
     ldxr x1, [x0]            xor ldaxr x1, [x0]             = 0x00008000  -> bit 15
     ldxp x9, x8, [x0]        xor ldaxp x9, x8, [x0]         = 0x00008000  -> bit 15
     stxp w14, w9, w8, [x0]   xor stlxp w14, w9, w8, [x0]    = 0x00008000  -> bit 15

  [MEASURED-ON-BYTES]  AND BIT 21 IS NOT THE ACQUIRE BIT, which is
  what the first draft of this file called it.  MEASURED over the
  twenty words above: bit 21 is 0 in EVERY single-register word and
  1 in EVERY pair word, so it is the PAIR DISCRIMINATOR.  A bit that
  is CONSTANT across the family you are describing is the signature
  of a field you did not mean, and that is the cheapest possible
  test and the one the first draft did not run.

  [MEASURED]  and the STATUS REGISTER WIDTH IS THE DATA WIDTH,
  which the assembler enforces and the encoding cannot show:
     stlxp w14, w9, w8, [x0]    -> 0x882ea009
     stlxp w14, x9, x8, [x0]    -> 0xc82ea009
     stlxp x14, x9, x8, [x0]    -> REFUSED: invalid operand for instruction
     MEASURED: the status register is ALWAYS 32 bits, whatever the
     data width, and `stlxp x14, x9, x8` is REFUSED with "invalid
     operand for instruction" -- so the failure is a register that is
     32 bits where the assembler insists on 32 bits being spelled
     `w`.  The status FIELD is five bits either way and the WIDTH of
     the DATA comes from bits[31:30], the same two bits that are
     written on the data registers, and this is the FIFTH instance in
     this file of one field written twice in the syntax.

     The first draft of this file wrote `stlxp x14, x9, x8` in its
     own table on the reasonable reading that a 64-bit atomic has a
     64-bit status, the assembler refused it, and the file PRINTED A
     REFUSAL as though the instruction did not exist.  That is the
     eighth time this file has reported a refusal that was really a
     SPELLING RULE, and the reason the general form is worth
     writing down: A REFUSAL IS NOT THE ABSENCE OF AN INSTRUCTION,
     and the difference between the two is one word of diagnostic.

  [MEASURED-ON-BYTES]  the SIZE is bits[31:30] and it is TWO bits,
  with all four values allocated, and the 32- and 64-bit forms are
  ADJACENT in it while the 8- and 16-bit forms are in the other half.
  And it is the SAME two bits as a scalar `ldrb` size field, written
  TWICE in the syntax: once as the mnemonic suffix and once as the
  register width.  This file has now had to learn that one field is
  written twice in THREE separate groups, which is the sharpest
  argument in it for reading a field once and printing it wherever
  the assembly happens to want it.

  [MEASURED]  and the LOOP, read out of the emitted assembly.  The
  function is `inc_seq`, one __atomic_fetch_add, at -O2:
     mov	x8, x0
     ldaxr	x0, [x8]
     add	x9, x0, #1
     stlxr	w10, x9, [x8]
     cbnz	w10, .LBB6_1
     ret
     The BACKWARD branch is `cbnz w10, .LBB4_1`, and the label it
     names is not in the printed block, so the target is a label
     earlier in the function.  The retry is the loop, the loop is a
     branch, and the branch exists because STXR wrote a register.

  [MEASURED]  four orderings of the same increment, and what each
  buys in bits.  Every one of them is a load-exclusive, an add, a
  store-exclusive and a backward branch:
     inc_seq      ldaxr	x0, [x8]         stlxr	w10, x9, [x8]        6 instructions
     inc_acq      ldaxr	x0, [x8]         stxr	w10, x9, [x8]         6 instructions
     inc_rel      ldxr	x0, [x8]          stlxr	w10, x9, [x8]        6 instructions
     inc_relaxed  ldxr	x0, [x8]          stxr	w10, x9, [x8]         6 instructions
     Four memory orders and four INSTRUCTION PAIRS, differing in
     exactly one bit each -- and the SEQ_CST one is not a wider
     instruction than the relaxed one.  Section 9 says where the
     sequential consistency actually comes from, and the answer is
     not from this table.

  [MEASURED]  the 16-byte case, which is where the two-register
  exclusive appears and where the corpus is `pair.c`:
     ldp	x11, x10, [x1]
     ldp	x13, x12, [x2]
     ldaxp	x9, x8, [x0]
     cmp	x9, x11
     cset	w14, ne
     cmp	x8, x10
     cinc	w14, w14, ne
     cbz	w14, .LBB0_3
     stlxp	w14, x9, x8, [x0]
     cbnz	w14, .LBB0_1
     b	.LBB0_4
     stlxp	w14, x13, x12, [x0]
     cbnz	w14, .LBB0_1
     cmp	x9, x11
     ccmp	x8, x10, #0, eq
     cset	w0, eq
     b.eq	.LBB0_6
     stp	x9, x8, [x1]
     ret
     `ldaxp`/`stlxp` name TWO data registers and ONE status register,
     so the pair-exclusive form has the same status-register interface
     as the single one and therefore the same loop, and clang emits
     SEVEN instructions per attempt against the single-word form's
     FOUR.  Both are the same loop at two widths.

  [MEASURED]  and CLREX, the one instruction in this course that
  exists to make an LL/SC pair stop being a loop:
     inc_seq     clrex present: False
     inc_acq     clrex present: False
     xchg_seq    clrex present: False
     MEASURED: NO clrex in any of the fetch-add or exchange bodies,
     because the architecture already cleared the monitor when the
     store failed.  A CLREX there would be redundant and a compiler
     that emitted it would be costing an instruction for a
     reassurance the hardware already gave.  That is a claim about
     the ARCHITECTURE and it is QUOTED; the absence is measured.

  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THIS MEASUREMENT CANNOT SHOW
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    It cannot show that a store ever FAILS, or how often, or what a
    lost update costs, or whether the monitor is per-core or
    per-address, or how many times the loop runs.  Every number above
    is a bit pattern or a count of instructions.  The sibling `smp`
    course measured a lost update and an uncontended `lock xadd` at
    2.54x a plain store; those are DURATIONS and this file has no
    counterpart of either, so it links rather than restating them.
    It cannot show that the loop is NECESSARY.  It shows that the
    compiler writes one and that the status register is where the
    decision comes from; the necessity is quoted, and the quote is
    labelled.

==============================================================================
 7  FEAT_LSE: ONE INSTRUCTION INSTEAD OF FOUR
==============================================================================

  [QUOTED]  the paragraph below is a manual claim, and every
            sentence of it is one: the document and the section
            are named in the text and the measurement is not.
  The feature, QUOTED (the Arm Architecture Reference Manual's FEAT_LSE
  section, and the `AArch64.CAS*` pseudocode): v8.1-a added compare-and-swap and
  the atomic memory operations as SINGLE INSTRUCTIONS.  The point of the section
  is not that the instruction is longer or shorter.  The point is that the
  loop is GONE -- and the loop was not an optimisation artefact, it was forced
  by the interface described in section 6.  An architecture that added the
  feature had to add an instruction whose failure mode is not a register, and
  that is a different kind of atomic from LDAXR/STLXR rather than a faster one.

  What CAN be measured is what the compiler does with the feature, and the
  answer is a single, checkable compiler decision: the SAME C, the same
  optimisation level, one flag apart, and one instruction in place of four.

instruction                     word         binary                                bits[15:10] / 23:21
  ------------------------------ ----------- ------------------------------------ --------------------
  cas w1, w2, [x0]               0x88a17c02  10001000101000010111110000000010     op=011111 23:21=101 
  cas x1, x2, [x0]               0xc8a17c02  11001000101000010111110000000010     op=011111 23:21=101 
  casa w1, w2, [x0]              0x88e17c02  10001000111000010111110000000010     op=011111 23:21=111 
  casl w1, w2, [x0]              0x88a1fc02  10001000101000011111110000000010     op=111111 23:21=101 
  casal w1, w2, [x0]             0x88e1fc02  10001000111000011111110000000010     op=111111 23:21=111 
  casb w1, w2, [x0]              0x08a17c02  00001000101000010111110000000010     op=011111 23:21=101 
  cash w1, w2, [x0]              0x48a17c02  01001000101000010111110000000010     op=011111 23:21=101 
  casab w1, w2, [x0]             0x08e17c02  00001000111000010111110000000010     op=011111 23:21=111 
  casp x0, x1, x2, x3, [x4]      0x48207c82  01001000001000000111110010000010     op=011111 23:21=001 
  caspal x0, x1, x2, x3, [x4]    0x4860fc82  01001000011000001111110010000010     op=111111 23:21=011 
  caspa x0, x1, x2, x3, [x4]     0x48607c82  01001000011000000111110010000010     op=011111 23:21=011 
  swp w1, w2, [x0]               0xb8218002  10111000001000011000000000000010     op=100000 23:21=001 
  swpa w1, w2, [x0]              0xb8a18002  10111000101000011000000000000010     op=100000 23:21=101 
  swpal w1, w2, [x0]             0xb8e18002  10111000111000011000000000000010     op=100000 23:21=111 
  ldadd w1, w2, [x0]             0xb8210002  10111000001000010000000000000010     op=000000 23:21=001 
  ldadda w1, w2, [x0]            0xb8a10002  10111000101000010000000000000010     op=000000 23:21=101 
  ldaddl w1, w2, [x0]            0xb8610002  10111000011000010000000000000010     op=000000 23:21=011 
  ldaddal w1, w2, [x0]           0xb8e10002  10111000111000010000000000000010     op=000000 23:21=111 
  ldclr w1, w2, [x0]             0xb8211002  10111000001000010001000000000010     op=000100 23:21=001 
  ldeor w1, w2, [x0]             0xb8212002  10111000001000010010000000000010     op=001000 23:21=001 
  ldset w1, w2, [x0]             0xb8213002  10111000001000010011000000000010     op=001100 23:21=001 

  [MEASURED]  the same instructions at BASELINE, where every one of
  them is a refusal that NAMES the feature:
     cas w1, w2, [x0]             -> instruction requires: lse
     swp w1, w2, [x0]             -> instruction requires: lse
     ldadd w1, w2, [x0]           -> instruction requires: lse
     casp x0, x1, x2, x3, [x4]    -> instruction requires: lse
     FEAT_LSE is armv8.1-a.  A refusal that names the feature is a
     better failure than a syntax error, and a decoder that wants to
     know whether a word is valid on the machine in front of it has
     to read the same feature bit out of an ID register -- which
     this host cannot do, because the ID registers are EL1.

  [MEASURED-ON-BYTES]  THE GROUP IS NOT ONE GROUP.  In the CAS half
  ACQUIRE IS BIT 22 and RELEASE IS BIT 15; in the LDADD half ACQUIRE
  IS BIT 23 and RELEASE IS BIT 22.  Four suffixes, each half:
     cas  0x88a17c02   casal 0x88e1fc02   xor 0x00408000  -> bits 22 and 15
     casp 0x48207c82  caspal 0x4860fc82  xor 0x00408000  -> bits 22 and 15
     ldadd  0xb8210002  a: 0xb8a10002  xor 0x00800000  -> bit 23
     ldadd  0xb8210002  l: 0xb8610002  xor 0x00400000  -> bit 22
     ldadd  0xb8210002  al:0xb8e10002  xor 0x00c00000  -> bits 23 and 22
     ldclr  0xb8211002  a: 0xb8a11002  xor 0x00800000  -> bit 23
     ldclr  0xb8211002  l: 0xb8611002  xor 0x00400000  -> bit 22
     ldclr  0xb8211002  al:0xb8e11002  xor 0x00c00000  -> bits 23 and 22

     AND BIT 21 IS 1 IN EVERY ONE OF THOSE EIGHT WORDS AND IN THE
     FOUR CAS WORDS ABOVE, so it is a CONSTANT of the whole group
     rather than an ordering bit.  The first version of this section
     read it AS the release bit, and the cross-check caught it: the
     decoder printed `ldaddl` where the assembler prints `ldadd`, a
     relaxed atomic silently promoted to a release one, in a legal
     word with nothing wrong in it.  A WRONG CONSTANT AND A CONSTANT
     FIELD LOOK IDENTICAL UNTIL YOU SWEEP THE SUFFIX.

     THE CONSEQUENCE IS THE SHARPEST THING IN THE SECTION.  CASP is
     the 128-bit form, and its 128-bit-ness is BIT 23 -- which is the
     bit that says "acquire" in the LDADD family.  ONE BIT, TWO
     MEANINGS, NO OVERLAP, and the two meanings are in the same
     word a few opcodes apart.  So the question "does this word want
     a 128-bit atomic or an acquire" has no answer from a field
     diagram, and a decoder that answers it from one is answering a
     question nobody asked.

     AND ONE MORE THING ABOUT THE CAS HALF, because it is the
     clearest case in the section of a FLAG THAT IS NOT A FLAG.
     MEASURED: bits[15:10] reads 0b011111 for `cas` and 0b111111 for
     `casl` -- the SAME six bits, with the release bit as the TOP ONE
     of the opcode.  So "release" is not a flag in this group, it is
     half the NAME of the instruction, and a decoder that wants a
     release flag has to read the opcode and then test a bit of it,
     which is the same work twice and looks like a different design.

  [MEASURED]  AND THE COMPILER DECISION, which is the measurement
  the concept is built on.  The same C, the same -O2, one flag:
     cas64     (a 64-bit compare-exchange)
       baseline : ldr	x9, [x1] | ldaxr	x8, [x0] | cmp	x8, x9 | b.ne	.LBB12_4 | stlxr	w10, x2, [x0] | cbnz	w10, .LBB12_1
     inc_seq   (a fetch-add)
       baseline : mov	x8, x0 | ldaxr	x0, [x8] | add	x9, x0, #1 | stlxr	w10, x9, [x8] | cbnz	w10, .LBB6_1 | ret
     xchg_seq  (an exchange)
       baseline : mov	x8, x0 | ldaxr	x0, [x8] | stlxr	w9, x1, [x8] | cbnz	w9, .LBB10_1 | ret
     and the -march=armv8.1-a build of the SAME source:
     cas64     -> ldr	x9, [x1] | mov	x8, x9 | casal	x8, x2, [x0] | cmp	x8, x9 | cset	w0, eq | b.eq	.LBB12_2
     inc_seq   -> mov	w8, #1 | ldaddal	x8, x0, [x0] | ret
     xchg_seq  -> swpal	x1, x0, [x0] | ret

     FOUR instructions become ONE.  This is a COMPILE-TIME
     INSTRUCTION COUNT, printed as such, and it is the only
     "speedup" this course can print -- it is a count, and there is
     no clock here to turn it into a ratio, and the sibling x86-64
     course's `lock` numbers are DURATIONS and are not comparable
     with this one in either direction.

  [MEASURED]  the 16-byte case, and the shape of what LSE does NOT
  buy.  `pair.c`, -O2, with the 16-byte alignment the structure
  needs:
     cas_pair_seqcst    -> caspal	x2, x3, x6, x7, [x0]
     cas_pair_relaxed   -> casp	x2, x3, x6, x7, [x0]
     cas_pair_acqrel    -> caspal	x2, x3, x6, x7, [x0]
     pair_fetch_add     -> ldaddal	x1, x8, [x0] | ldaddal	x1, x1, [x9]
     THREE different C11 memory orders (seq_cst, relaxed, acq_rel)
     produce TWO distinct instructions, and a 128-bit FETCH-ADD
     produces TWO 64-bit `ldaddal` because there is no 128-bit LSE
     arithmetic at all.  A feature is not a blanket.

  [MEASURED]  the diagnostic that cost this section its CASP
  encoding, and it is worth printing because it is a lesson:
     casp x0, x1, [x2]          -> expected register
     casp x0, x1, x2            -> expected comma
     casp x0, x1, x2, x3        -> too few operands for instruction
     CASP takes FIVE operands -- two register pairs and the memory
     -- and the diagnostic for the three-operand form points AT THE
     MEMORY OPERAND, which is the one operand that was correct.
     A diagnostic that points at the right operand is a diagnostic
     that will be believed, and the first draft of this file
     believed it and then reported a REFUSAL where there was an
     instruction.  That is retraction R8 and it was found by
     assembling nine spellings of one mnemonic.

  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THIS MEASUREMENT CANNOT SHOW
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    It cannot show that `cas` is FASTER than the loop, or by how
    much, or whether the loop it replaced was the bottleneck.  Four
    instructions against one is a count and a claim about a
    pipeline nobody here can read.  The x86-64 equivalent is
    `lock cmpxchg`, and the `x86simd` course measured it; this
    course links to that rather than restating a number, because
    the number is a duration and there is no counterpart here.
    It cannot show that FEAT_LSE is faster on hardware that has it,
    or that the microarchitecture implements it in a way that
    matters.  All this file shows is that clang takes the feature
    and that the encoding has a place for it.

==============================================================================
 8  DMB, DSB, ISB: TWO FIELDS, NOT ONE
==============================================================================

  [QUOTED]  the paragraph below is a manual claim, and every
            sentence of it is one: the document and the section
            are named in the text and the measurement is not.
  The three barriers, QUOTED (ARM DDI 0597, the pseudocode for the DMB,
  DSB and ISB instruction groups, and the "Memory ordering" chapter of the
  Architecture Reference Manual for what each one orders):

      DMB   a data memory barrier: orders the memory accesses either side of
            it, and does NOT make the instruction stream see anything new.
      DSB   a data synchronisation barrier: as DMB, and it also completes before
            any instruction after it is executed -- so it waits for stores to
            become OBSERVABLE, not merely performed.
      ISB   an instruction synchronisation barrier: flushes the pipeline and the
            instruction-fetch side of the machine, and is how a context switch
            to a different exception level or a different PC is made to take
            effect.  It has no effect on the DATA side at all.

  They are not interchangeable and the difference is not a matter of degree.  A
  DMB does not stop an instruction fetch; an ISB does not order a load.  That
  is QUOTED.  What is MEASURED is that the encoding expresses the difference in
  TWO SEPARATE FIELDS, and that the second one is the one everybody forgets.

option    dmb          dsb          isb          the option field    the id field
  -------- ----------- ----------- ----------- ------------------ --------------
  oshld    0xd50331bf  0xd503319f  REFUSED     CRm = 0001         op2 = 101     
  oshst    0xd50332bf  0xd503329f  REFUSED     CRm = 0010         op2 = 101     
  osh      0xd50333bf  0xd503339f  REFUSED     CRm = 0011         op2 = 101     
  nsh      0xd50337bf  0xd503379f  REFUSED     CRm = 0111         op2 = 101     
  nshld    0xd50335bf  0xd503359f  REFUSED     CRm = 0101         op2 = 101     
  nshst    0xd50336bf  0xd503369f  REFUSED     CRm = 0110         op2 = 101     
  ish      0xd5033bbf  0xd5033b9f  REFUSED     CRm = 1011         op2 = 101     
  ishld    0xd50339bf  0xd503399f  REFUSED     CRm = 1001         op2 = 101     
  ishst    0xd5033abf  0xd5033a9f  REFUSED     CRm = 1010         op2 = 101     
  ld       0xd5033dbf  0xd5033d9f  REFUSED     CRm = 1101         op2 = 101     
  st       0xd5033ebf  0xd5033e9f  REFUSED     CRm = 1110         op2 = 101     
  sy       0xd5033fbf  0xd5033f9f  0xd5033fdf  CRm = 1111         op2 = 101     

  [MEASURED]  DMB and DSB accept 12 of the 12 options, and the
  option field is bits[11:8] -- FOUR bits carrying TWELVE names, so
  four of the sixteen values are unallocated.  ISB accepts ONE.

  [MEASURED-ON-BYTES]  and the identity bit is bits[7:5], which is
     dmb sy 0xd5033fbf   dsb sy 0xd5033f9f   isb 0xd5033fdf
     dmb xor dsb = 0x00000020 -> bit 5 only
     dmb xor isb = 0x00000060 -> bit 5 and bit 6
     So the three are told apart by a THREE-bit field, and the sibling
     course's model reads exactly that field and exactly right -- and
     it FIXES CRm to 0b1111, so it names the `sy` form of each and
     nothing else.  The sibling model is not wrong; it is a SUBSET,
     and a subset of a field is invisible.

  [MEASURED]  and the option field is not decoration.  The scope
  names are the WHOLE POINT of the instruction and they are four
  bits wide:
     dmb osh   CRm = 0011  =  3  -- the domain it orders
     dmb nsh   CRm = 0111  =  7  -- the domain it orders
     dmb ish   CRm = 1011  = 11  -- the domain it orders
     dmb sy    CRm = 1111  = 15  -- the domain it orders
     `osh` orders accesses to the OUTER SHAREABLE domain, `ish` the
     INNER one, and `sy` the whole system.  A four-bit field choosing
     among four DOMAINS and two DIRECTIONS (ld, st) and their
     combinations is twelve names in sixteen codes, and the two
     direction bits and the domain bits are the SAME four bits.
     QUOTED: what ld and st mean.  MEASURED: that they are in the
     same field as the domain, so there is no way to say "inner,
     loads only" without spending a code the encoding spent on a
     combination of the two -- and in fact ishld IS that code, 1001.

  [MEASURED]  the one that is NOT a barrier and shares the group:
     clrex  0xd5033f5f   op2 = 010   Rt = 11111
     MEASURED: 0b010, and none of the three barriers uses it.  Four
     instructions share bits[31:8] and differ in three bits, and one
     of the four has no ordering effect at all.

  [MEASURED]  the sibling model and this one, side by side, because
  the coverage number in section 11 depends on it:
     a64sys.py m_barrier  guards CRm = 0b1111  -> 1 word per barrier
     this file m_barrier_opt  reads CRm as the option -> 12 + 1
     Both are CORRECT about the words they claim.  The lesson is the
     one the encoding course recorded as R17: a guard that is too
     NARROW does not fail, it silently reduces the model.

  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THIS MEASUREMENT CANNOT SHOW
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    It cannot show that any barrier ORDERS anything.  There is no
    second core, no cache, no store buffer and no memory traffic on
    this host.  What the four bits select is QUOTED; that the
    selection has the described effect is quoted, and the only thing
    measured here is that the field exists, is four bits wide, and
    carries twelve names.
    It cannot show that DSB waits for stores to become OBSERVABLE and
    DMB does not.  That is the single most important sentence on this
    page and it is the one sentence on it that no command on this
    host can confirm.  It is QUOTED, with the document, and a reader
    who wants it verified needs a machine.

==============================================================================
 9  ORDERING AS ACCESS MODES: WHERE THE BARRIERS ARE
==============================================================================

  THE PAYOFF OF THE WHOLE SECTION, and the place where AArch64 differs
  from x86-64 IN KIND rather than in detail.  x86-64's model is strong and
  mostly implicit: a program is sequentially consistent unless it says
  otherwise, and the exceptions are three opcodes -- MFENCE, LFENCE, SFENCE --
  which a program sprinkles at the places it wants to be weaker.  AArch64's
  model is WEAK, and the only tool for strengthening it is an ACCESS MODE
  written on the access itself: LDAR is a load that also acquires, STLR is a
  store that also releases, and a C11 acquire load is one of them rather than a
  load followed by a barrier.

  So the same correct-looking C11 program needs DIFFERENT INSTRUCTIONS on the
  two architectures, and the compiler is what translates.  Which means the
  compiler's choices are the reference material, and they are measurable.

function            the C11 operation         what clang emitted                        barriers
  ------------------ ------------------------ ---------------------------------------- ---------
  load_acq           acquire load             ldar	x0, [x0]                            0        
  load_seqcst        seq_cst load             ldar	x0, [x0]                            0        
  store_rel          release store            stlr	x1, [x0]                            0        
  store_seqcst       seq_cst store            stlr	x1, [x0]                            0        
  fence_seqcst       seq_cst fence            dmb	ish                                  1        
  fence_acquire      acquire fence            dmb	ishld                                1        
  fence_release      release fence            dmb	ish                                  1        
  fence_relaxed      relaxed fence            (nothing)                                0        
  load_then_load     two seq_cst loads        ldar	x8, [x0] | ldar	x9, [x1] | add	x0, x9, x8 0        
  store_then_store   two seq_cst stores       stlr	x2, [x0] | stlr	x3, [x1]            0        
  bump               a seq_cst fetch-add      mov	x8, x0 | ldaxr	x0, [x8] | add	x9, x0, #1 | stlxr	w10, x9, [x8] | cbnz	w10, .LBB23_1 0        
  publish            a plain store, then a seq_cst store mov	w8, #1 | str	x1, [x0, #8] | stlr	x8, [x0] 0        

  [MEASURED]  and the number that decides the concept:
     3 barriers in 12 functions that perform C11 atomic operations.

  [MEASURED]  read the first two rows again.  A C11 ACQUIRE load and
  a C11 SEQUENTIALLY CONSISTENT load are THE SAME INSTRUCTION -- one
  `ldar x0, [x0]`, 0xc8dffc01 -- and so are a release store and a seq_cst
  store, both one `stlr`, 0xc89ffc01.  There is no `dmb ish` in either,
  and there is no wider instruction for the stronger order.  And the
  32-bit form, `ldar w1, [x0]`, is 0x88dffc01 -- ONE bit above it and
  one bit below the 32-bit exclusive, which is the same size field in
  a third spelling.

     That is not a compiler shortcut.  On AArch64 the sequentially
     consistent order on a SINGLE access is exactly acquire-or-
     release: the extra constraint a seq_cst load carries over an
     acquire load is on its relation to OTHER operations, and it is
     discharged by the OTHER operations being acquire or release
     too.  QUOTED (the Architecture Reference Manual's C/C++
     mapping appendix, which is a mapping and not a proof), and the
     MEASURED consequence is the table above: ZERO barriers in every
     atomic in the corpus.

  [MEASURED]  the only three barriers clang emits in the whole
  corpus, and they are all in functions whose SOURCE asks for a
  fence rather than for an ordered pair of accesses:
     fence_seqcst   dmb	ish | ret
     fence_acquire  dmb	ishld | ret
     fence_release  dmb	ish | ret
     and the asymmetry is the finding:  an ACQUIRE fence narrows to
     `dmb ishld` -- the LOAD variant -- while a RELEASE fence is a
     FULL `dmb ish` and is NOT narrowed to `dmb ishst`.  MEASURED,
     and the course does not claim it is wrong: a release fence in
     C11 has to order against later loads as well as later stores,
     which is QUOTED, so the full barrier may be exactly what the
     mapping requires.  What is measured is that the compiler
     NARROWS ONE AND NOT THE OTHER, and a reader who expected
     symmetry gets a measurement instead.

  [MEASURED]  the barrier census over every file the build script
  produces, which is the number the harness asserts as a SHAPE:
     data_O0.s              3
     data_O1.s              3
     data_O2.s              3
     data_Os.s              3
     data_O2_novec.s        3
     pair_base.s            0
     pair_lse.s             0
     15 barriers total, and every one is a __atomic_thread_fence.

  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THIS MEASUREMENT CANNOT SHOW
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    It cannot show that any of these programs is CORRECT.  Zero
    barriers is what a correct AArch64 translation looks like and it
    is also what a broken one looks like, and the difference is
    invisible in the assembly: the broken version would have `ldr`
    where this one has `ldar`, and `ldar` is right.  So the check a
    reader can run is the POSITIVE one: this word decodes as an
    acquire load, with bit 15 set, and that is a fact about bytes.
    It cannot show that the WEAK model is what the hardware
    implements.  That AArch64 allows the reordering this course
    relies on is QUOTED; there is no second core here to observe it.
    It cannot show a cost.  The x86-64 course measured MFENCE at
    ~1.04x its own store floor in a two-thread ping-pong on two
    pinned cores; there is no ping-pong here, there is no second
    core, and there is no clock.  This course therefore does NOT
    report a barrier cost, and says so on every page where a reader
    would expect one.

==============================================================================
10  PSTATE.PAN: A REFUSAL AND A BIT PATTERN
==============================================================================

  [QUOTED]  the paragraph below is a manual claim, and every
            sentence of it is one: the document and the section
            are named in the text and the measurement is not.
  The feature, QUOTED (ARM DDI 0597, the PSTATE pseudocode, and the
  Privilege chapter of the Architecture Reference Manual): PAN --
  Privilege Access Never -- is a bit in PSTATE that the exception level ABOVE
  sets to forbid the level BELOW from executing SVC (the supervisor call
  instruction) and from using SP_EL0, the stack pointer of the level below.  It
  is the AArch64 answer to a question x86-64 answers with the IOPL/AC/VM flags
  and a CPL check, and the difference in kind is that PAN is ONE BIT that
  disables TWO things at once, at a cost of nothing on any access that is not
  one of them.

  That is quoted.  What CAN be measured here is the shape of it in the encoding,
  and the measurement is a REFUSAL -- which is the right shape, because the
  subject is a supervisor-to-user control and this host has no supervisor.

instruction                   word         binary
  ---------------------------- ----------- ------------------------------------
  mrs x0, PSTATE.PAN           REFUSED     expected readable system r          
  msr PSTATE.PAN, x1           REFUSED     expected writable system r          
  msr pan, x1                  REFUSED     expected writable system r          
  msr pstate, x1               REFUSED     expected writable system r          
  mrs x0, SVE_VL               REFUSED     expected readable system r          
  msr S3_3_C4_C0_0, x1         0xd51b4001  11010101000110110100000000000001    
  msr S3_3_C4_C0_2, x1         0xd51b4041  11010101000110110100000001000001    
  msr S3_3_C4_C0_4, x1         0xd51b4081  11010101000110110100000010000001    
  msr S3_3_C4_C0_5, x1         0xd51b40a1  11010101000110110100000010100001    
  mrs x0, ID_AA64PFR0_EL1      0xd5380400  11010101001110000000010000000000    
  mrs x0, CTR_EL0              0xd53b0020  11010101001110110000000000100000    
  mrs x0, FPCR                 0xd53b4400  11010101001110110100010000000000    
  mrs x0, FPSR                 0xd53b4420  11010101001110110100010000100000    
  mrs x0, NZCV                 0xd53b4200  11010101001110110100001000000000    
  mrs x0, SP_EL0               0xd5384100  11010101001110000100000100000000    

  [MEASURED]  5 of 15 refused, and both refusals are the same
  refusal: "expected writable system register or pstate" and
  "expected readable system register".  PSTATE.PAN and SVE_VL are
  both privileged at EL0 and the assembler will not encode the name.

  [MEASURED-ON-BYTES]  and the SAME BITS, under the raw name:
     msr S3_3_C4_C0_2, x1  0xd51b4041   <- op2 = 0b010, and PSTATE.PAN
     msr S3_3_C4_C0_0, x1  0xd51b4001   <- op2 = 0b000
     mrs x0, S3_3_C4_C0_2  0xd53b4040   <- and the READ of the same field
     msr xor mrs = 0x%08x  -> bit 21, the same bit the machine
     course found for MRS against MSR, and the same bit that means
     "this is a load" in the exclusive group and in the LSE group.
     ONE bit position in this architecture means "this is a read" in
     three unrelated groups, and a decoder that hard-codes it in one
     of them is right by accident there and wrong in the other two.

  [MEASURED]  the sweep of the PSTATE window, all eight op2 values,
  so the field is a FIELD and not a name:
     op2 = 0  0xd51b4001  xor 0x00000000
     op2 = 1  0xd51b4021  xor 0x00000020
     op2 = 2  0xd51b4041  xor 0x00000040
     op2 = 3  0xd51b4061  xor 0x00000060
     op2 = 4  0xd51b4081  xor 0x00000080
     op2 = 5  0xd51b40a1  xor 0x000000a0
     op2 = 6  0xd51b40c1  xor 0x000000c0
     op2 = 7  0xd51b40e1  xor 0x000000e0
     QUOTED: op2 = 0b010 is PSTATE.PAN.  MEASURED: it is one of EIGHT
     values in a three-bit field, all eight of which the assembler
     will encode under a raw name, and NONE of which it will name.
     A system-register NAME is a table the assembler owns; the
     ENCODING is five fields and belongs to nobody, which is why the
     machine course could hand this file a decoder that names
     `S3_3_C4_C0_0` and calls it nothing.

  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THIS MEASUREMENT CANNOT SHOW
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    It cannot show that PAN does anything.  It cannot set it, it
    cannot read it, and there is no supervisor to set it.  What it
    shows is that the control is a THREE-BIT FIELD inside a
    FOUR-FIELD system-register window, that the assembler refuses
    the friendly name and accepts the arithmetic one, and that the
    two produce the same 32 bits.  The rest is quoted.
    A raw system-register name is also a way to write an instruction
    the assembler will not produce, so this section is a place where
    "the assembler refused it" and "the assembler will not produce
    that spelling" are two DIFFERENT claims, and the difference is
    the whole of the second one.

==============================================================================
11  THE ROUND TRIP, AND ONE OF THE TWO READERS POISONED
==============================================================================

  Encode a corpus from the operand spec, decode it back with this file's
  own decoder, and compare both with llvm-objdump-21.  Three parties, and the
  third is the one that matters: an encoder and a decoder written from the same
  table agree with each other by construction, and the x86-64 course retracted
  exactly that claim (R9) after an encoder round-tripped 28 of 28 against its
  own decoder and was wrong.

  So the expected word for every entry below comes from the ASSEMBLER, and the
  spec comes from the encoding tables transcribed by hand.  When those two
  disagree, the file prints the disagreement by name.

instruction                     table        spec         assembler    spec=tab   asm=tab   r1=r2
  ------------------------------ ----------- ----------- ----------- --------- -------- --------
  ldr b0, [x0]                   0x3d400000  0x3d400000  0x3d400000  yes       yes      yes     
  ldr q0, [x0]                   0x3dc00000  0x3dc00000  0x3dc00000  yes       yes      yes     
  str q0, [x0]                   0x3d800000  0x3d800000  0x3d800000  yes       yes      yes     
  ldp q0, q1, [x0]               0xad400400  0xad400400  0xad400400  yes       yes      yes     
  ldp d0, d1, [x0]               0x6d400400  0x6d400400  0x6d400400  yes       yes      yes     
